## Project layout

The repository root **is** the site's web root — whatever static host serves this folder
serves these files unchanged, so nothing that is not part of the site belongs at the top
level.

- `index.html` — the home page and the entry point the pre-commit hook checks for.
- `404.html` — served by most static hosts for unknown paths.
- `styles.css` — the single stylesheet, linked from every page.
- `main.js` — progressive-enhancement JavaScript, loaded with `defer`. The site must still
  render without it.
- `assets/` — images, fonts, icons and anything else the pages reference. `favicon.svg`
  lives here.

The only other top-level entries are the sandbox machinery cdforge manages
(`.devcontainer/`, `.githooks/`, `.claude/`) and the project documents (`README.md`,
`CLAUDE.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `LICENSE`).

## Running things

- `python3 -m http.server 8000` — from the repository root, serve the site for preview on
  http://localhost:8000 (port 8000 is forwarded from the devcontainer). Nothing to install
  or build first.
- There is no test suite. `.githooks/pre-commit` only checks that `index.html` exists.

## Conventions

- Hand-write HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Do **not** introduce a build step, a bundler, a CSS
  or JS framework, or a `package.json` — the point of this project is that the folder is
  deployed exactly as it is.
- Every new page is a top-level `*.html` file that links `styles.css` and, if it needs it,
  `main.js`. Keep the `<head>` (charset, viewport, title, stylesheet, favicon) consistent
  across pages.
- Reference assets and other pages with **relative** paths (`assets/logo.svg`,
  `./about.html`) so the site works whether it is served from a domain root or a sub-path.
- Write semantic, valid HTML: one `<h1>` per page, landmark elements (`<header>`, `<nav>`,
  `<main>`, `<footer>`), `alt` text on every image.
- Keep `styles.css` organised with plain CSS custom properties for colours and spacing;
  prefer modern CSS (flexbox, grid, `clamp()`) over adding tooling.
